House Robber II

Intuition
Because the houses are arranged in a circle, robbing the first house means you cannot rob the last house, and vice versa. This reduces the circular problem into two linear House Robber subproblems: one considering houses from the first to the second-to-last house (index 0 to n-2), and the other considering houses from the second to the last house (index 1 to n-1). The maximum of these two scenarios is the final answer.

Approach
Use the linear House Robber dynamic programming logic as a helper function across two distinct ranges:

Handle the edge case where the array has only 1 house by directly returning its value.

Helper function takes a range [l, r] and computes the maximum robbery amount using two variables (a and b) for constant space.

Run the helper for range [0, n - 2] (excluding the last house) and range [1, n - 1] (excluding the first house).

Return the maximum result between the two runs.

Algo

Check if the array length n is 1. If so, return nums[0].

Define a helper function that takes nums and boundaries l and r:
a. Initialize a = 0 and b = 0.
b. Loop index i from l to r:

Compute c = max(b, a + nums[i]).

Set a = b.

Set b = c.
c. Return b.

Compute result1 = helper(nums, 0, n - 2).

Compute result2 = helper(nums, 1, n - 1).

Return max(result1, result2).

Code

class Solution {
int helper(vector& nums, int l, int r) {
int a = 0, b = 0;
for (int i = l; i <= r; ++i) {
int c = max(b, a + nums[i]);
a = b;
b = c;
}
return b;
}
public:
int rob(vector& nums) {
int n = nums.size();
if (n == 1) return nums[0];
return max(helper(nums, 0, n - 2), helper(nums, 1, n - 1));
}
};

Time Complexity
O(n): The helper function runs twice over subarrays of length n - 1, resulting in linear O(n) total runtime with O(1) operations per iteration.

Space Complexity
O(1): Only a few primitive integer variables (a, b, c, n) are used, requiring no dynamic heap memory or extra array allocations.

Why the optimal solution is better than the brute-force approach
A brute-force recursive backtracking solution would explore all possible non-adjacent combinations around the circle, yielding an exponential O(2^n) time complexity that causes Time Limit Exceeded (TLE) errors. The DP approach breaks the circular dependency into two linear passes, achieving an optimal O(n) runtime and O(1) auxiliary space.
